--- CRITERION 6: did R13 remove an assertion it should have kept? --- NOT MET
YES, and the persisted check does NOT strictly dominate the one that was dropped.
`grep -rn "\.Verdict" internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/*_test.go` -> after R13 there is NO
assertion anywhere in the package on resolveTransferOutput.Verdict, the field the tool reports
back to the model/user. Before R13 it was asserted in both tests (HEAD resolve_test.go:97-98
and HEAD browser_approval_test.go:220-221).
Proof it is a real coverage loss, not a theoretical one -- scratch r13echo, single-line
mutation of the OUTPUT field only, write left correct:
    resolve.go:236   Verdict: string(v),   ->   Verdict: "MUTATED-echo-only",
  post-R13 tests:  `go test -count=1 ./...` -> NO failing test in the entire module.
  pre-R13 tests (same mutation, `git checkout HEAD -- <the two test files>`, diff vs HEAD is
  resolve.go only) -> --- FAIL: TestResolveTransferApprovedInBrowserRecordsTheDescribedVerdict
                      --- FAIL: TestResolveTransferSecondCallWithTokenWrites
So R13 closed the write-side hole and simultaneously opened a display-side one: a build in
which resolve_transfer persists "confirm" but TELLS the model it recorded "reject" now ships
green. That is the same family of defect R4 exists to prevent (what the human was shown vs
what the machine did), on the reporting side. The two checks are orthogonal; the persisted one
does not subsume the echo one.
REMEDIATION (one line per test, no production change): keep the original
`if out.Verdict != "confirm" { ... }` assertion IN ADDITION to the persisted check in both
TestResolveTransferApprovedInBrowserRecordsTheDescribedVerdict and
TestResolveTransferSecondCallWithTokenWrites, with the comment adjusted to say the output
field is asserted as a report-to-caller check while the persisted value is the authority.
NOTE FOR ADJUDICATION: the brief's criterion 1 says to assert the persisted verdict "not the
echoed output field", which the worker may reasonably have read as an instruction to delete
the old check. I record the fact and its measured consequence; the lead decides whether the
brief sanctioned the deletion.

